16:11:16 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s a sharp question—you’re cutting right through the nuance and asking where the line really is. It deserves a direct answer. In a sense, you’re right: **if by “developing” you mean the genetically timed, structural maturation of the brain’s basic information-processing framework, then by the mid‑20s, that process is basically finished.** The brain has settled into its adult architecture. You could call that “done,” and many scientists do use that kind of language for public communication. But here’s why the “lifelong plasticity” part isn’t just minor tweaking—and why it genuinely counts as ongoing development in a broader sense. ### 1. The scope of plasticity is far from minor When we talk about plasticity, we’re not just talking about tiny chemical nudges. We’re talking about the physical changes that underlie everything you learn and every skill you master as an adult: - Learning a new language as an adult physically thickens regions in the left parietal cortex. - London taxi drivers who memorize “The Knowledge” grow a measurably larger posterior hippocampus. - Learning to juggle increases gray matter in visual-motor areas in just weeks. - After a stroke, if a piece of cortex dies, the brain can reorganize so that a different region takes over the lost function entirely—something that can happen decades beyond 25. These aren’t metaphors. They are structural, cellular-level reorganizations—synapses forming and dying, neurons tuning their properties, even, in a few areas, new neurons being born. That is development in a very real sense: the transformation of the brain’s circuits to meet new demands. It’s just not a pre-programmed, maturational timeline anymore; it’s experience-driven. ### 2. The “wiring” isn’t a fixed thing you complete When we say the prefrontal cortex’s wiring reaches its “mature configuration,” we mean that the *default blueprint* is fully installed—the massive overproduction of synapses and the subsequent pruning