16:12:14 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s a great analogy, and it forces a much more precise answer. I’m going to take the tree seriously, because it’s a better metaphor than you might realize—and it actually shows why the brain’s situation is different. You’re right: **once a tree’s trunk and primary branches have grown, that structural framework is done.** A mature oak won’t spontaneously sprout a new main trunk. Swaying, losing leaves, even growing new twigs within the existing scaffold—none of that counts as “still growing the tree.” If we apply that *exact* standard to the brain, then yes: **the genetically scripted construction of the brain’s large-scale, long-range connectivity—the equivalent of the trunk and major branches—is essentially complete by the mid-20s.** That’s the pruning and myelination phase I described. That’s the part that underlies impulse control and risk assessment. That’s the part car insurance cares about. So under your definition, brain *development* in the strict, constructional sense is indeed finished. But here’s where the tree analogy breaks down—and why neuroscience insists on a broader idea of “development” long after 25. --- ### 1. A tree can’t grow a whole new functional branch from old trunk wood. Your brain actually can. Once an oak’s trunk is lignified, you won’t get a new primary limb bursting out of old bark. In the brain, however, experience doesn’t just tweak minor twigs—it can **repurpose entire cortical neighborhoods**, grow new neuron populations (in the hippocampus), and even reorganize sensory maps. For instance: - **London taxi drivers** don’t just “use” their spatial memory better; they grow a measurably larger posterior hippocampus, a literal structural enlargement in an area that wasn’t that big before. That’s like a tree not just swaying, but increasing the diameter of one of its main limbs in response to a persistent wind direction. Trees don’t do that; brains do. - **After a stroke**, if a chunk of brain responsible for speech dies, neighboring regions (or even the opposite hemisphere) can take over that function in ways that require new long-range connections. That’s not just swaying; it’s re-routing the entire flow of information. That’s trunk-level change occurring decades after 25. So while the *initial blueprint* is set, the brain retains the ability to **architecturally remodel** its core structures in response to demand. That goes well beyond “using” the static framework. ### 2. The “trunk” itself can change thickness and character—something a tree’s trunk largely doesn’t do after maturity. Even white-matter tracts (the cabling between regions) can continue to myelinate and alter their microstructure into your 30s and beyond, especially with intense training (say, learning to read as an adult, or mastering a musical instrument). The thickness of a tree trunk is set; the connectivity “trunk lines” of your brain aren’t. --- ### So what’s really going on? You’ve put your finger on a definitional split: - If you define **development** as the **innate, biological unfolding of a pre-determined physical structure**, then you’re absolutely right—the brain stops developing in the mid-20s. This is the definition most biologists use for “maturation,” and it’s why I talked about the “mature adult configuration.” By that definition, adult plasticity is literally just learning, not development. - If you define **development** as any **structural adaptation that expands future possibilities**, then the brain never stops. This is the sense in which neuroscientists use phrases like “lifelong brain development” or “adult neurogenesis as brain development.” It’s a different, but legitimate, use of the word. Your tree analogy brilliantly captures the first definition. And I suspect you’re unwilling to call after-25 changes “development” because you reserve that word for the construction of the basic hardware. I think that’s a completely coherent position. --- ### A better analogy might be: a